    "result_text": "Done. The full suite (the single `AcceptanceTest`) is green with **BUILD SUCCESS** \u2014 2 tests, 0 failures.\n\n## What I changed\n\nOnly `Locality.java` \u2014 one file, one existing method touched (`of`) plus one new small private helper.\n\nThe locality derivation now:\n1. **Tries the postcode range first** \u2014 a 4-digit postcode maps NSW `2000\u20132099`, VIC `3000\u20133099`, QLD `4000\u20134099`.\n2. **Falls back to the city-to-region table** when the postcode is absent or in no known range.\n\nKnown cities still resolve identically (Sydney carries postcode 2000 \u2192 NSW), while an unknown city with a VIC-range postcode 3000 now correctly yields VIC \u2014 proving postcode precedence.\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\n- **One file touched.**\n- The `Locality` class had essentially no other-method complexity (`WMC_other \u2248 1`), so the whole cost multiplier stays minimal.\n- The new range logic lives in a separate cohesive helper `fromPostcode(...)` rather than inflating `of`, so `of` stays a simple 4-line dispatcher. Adding behaviour as a small new unit \u2014 instead of growing an existing complex method \u2014 is exactly the low-cost lever.\n\nThe postcode is validated with a `\\d{4}` guard before parsing, so malformed values simply fall through to the city table.",
